## Words or meaning

<At levels="simple">

In <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />, retrieval found the cat rhyme by meaning. Search
engines have long worked another way: a <GlossaryPopover term="keyword-search">keyword search</GlossaryPopover>
looks for the question's own words. Each finds things the other misses. Try a few questions about
the same chat:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

In <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" />, retrieval found the cat rhyme by meaning. Search
engines have long worked another way: a <GlossaryPopover term="keyword-search">keyword search</GlossaryPopover>
scores each text by the question's own words it contains, after dropping common ones like "the" and "which". The usual
method, BM25, gives more credit for words that are rare among all the texts and for using them
more often, and less for long texts.<Cite id="robertson2009" /> It is exact: it finds a name, or
a code like an error number, that an embedding blurs. Anthropic's example is a search for "Error
code TS-999", where embeddings may find pages about error codes in general and miss the one about
TS-999.<Cite id="anthropic-contextual-retrieval" /> But it knows nothing of meaning: "kitty"
doesn't match "cat". Try a few questions about the same chat:

</At>

<SearchCompare />

<At levels="simple">

Words found Sam, Leo, and the crown, which meaning missed, but found nothing for "kitty", which
meaning got right. So many systems run both searches and combine the two lists, giving each text
points for how high it comes in each.<Cite id="cormack2009" /> Here that picked the right exchange
for 4 of the 5 questions; words alone got 4 and meaning alone 2.

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Words found Sam, Leo, and the crown, which meaning missed: names carry little meaning, and the
crown pulled meaning toward Humpty Dumpty and the king's men. Meaning found "kitty", a word in no
exchange. So many systems run both searches and merge the two lists by rank: each text gets points
for how high it comes in each list, so one near the top of either does well.<Cite id="cormack2009" />
Anthropic found that adding keyword search to embeddings missed fewer of the right
passages.<Cite id="anthropic-contextual-retrieval" /> Here the merged list put the right exchange
first for 4 of the 5 questions, words alone for 4, and meaning alone for 2. It still missed Leo:
meaning ranked the star rhyme first and words second, which beat Leo's rhyme, first by words but
only third by meaning.

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### Scoring words and ranks

For a question with words $q$, BM25 scores a text $D$ as

$$
\sum_{q} \text{idf}(q)\,\frac{f(q, D)\,(k_1 + 1)}{f(q, D) + k_1\left(1 - b + b\,\frac{|D|}{\text{avgdl}}\right)},
$$

where $f(q, D)$ counts the word in the text, $|D|$ is the text's length in words, avgdl the
average length, and $k_1 = 1.2$ and $b = 0.75$ are the usual settings.<Cite id="robertson2009" />
The count saturates: a word used five times scores less than five times one use. The weight
$\text{idf}(q) = \ln\!\left(1 + \frac{N - n + 0.5}{n + 0.5}\right)$, for a word in $n$ of the $N$
texts, is highest for words in only one; the 1 inside the logarithm keeps a word found in every
text from counting against a match.

Reciprocal rank fusion merges the lists using only ranks, so it needn't compare BM25 scores with
cosines: each text scores $\sum 1/(k + r)$ over the lists that rank it, at rank $r$, with
$k = 60$.<Cite id="cormack2009" /> For Leo's question the star rhyme gets
$1/62 + 1/61 \approx 0.0325$ and Leo's rhyme $1/61 + 1/63 \approx 0.0323$.

</At>

## Remembering between chats

<At levels="simple">

All of that happens inside one chat. A new chat starts with an empty prompt, and the model
remembers nothing of the last one. Apps with a memory feature save notes outside the model and put
them into the next chat's prompt.<Cite id="openai-memory" /> Here the harness ends the long chat
by asking the model to write notes, in two ways:

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

All of that happens inside one conversation. A new chat starts with an empty prompt, and the
model, whose weights don't change as you talk, remembers nothing of the last one. Memory features
fill the gap the way the strategies in <ChapterRef to="managing-the-context" /> do: the harness saves notes outside the model and
puts them into the next chat's prompt. ChatGPT, for example, saves things you ask it to remember
and details it picks up itself, and later began drawing on past chats too.<Cite id="openai-memory" />
Here the harness ends the long chat by asking the model to write notes, in two ways:

</At>

<MemoryNotes />

<At levels="simple">

Asked openly, the model wrote only about the last child, Mei, and made up the rest, such as plans
for an astronomy camp. With those notes, the new chat couldn't answer any question. Asked for each
child and their rhyme, it did better but mixed up Sam's and Mei's rhymes, so the new chat said Sam
loves "Twinkle, twinkle" without any doubt. A wrong note is repeated in every chat after, which is
why apps let you see and delete what they remember.<Cite id="openai-memory" />

</At>
<At levels="standard deep">

Without notes, the new chat knows nothing and says so. Asked openly for notes about the user, the
model wrote only about the last child, Mei, and filled the rest with things that never came up:
an interest in astronomy, books about space, an astronomy camp next summer. With those notes it
still couldn't answer any of the three questions. Asked for each child and the rhyme they love, it
got three right but gave Sam the star rhyme, which was Mei's, gave Mei Leo's clock rhyme, and left
out Humpty Dumpty. The new chat then said Sam loves "Twinkle, twinkle", with no sign of doubt.

So what gets written down matters, and a wrong note is worse than a wrong reply: it is repeated in
every chat after. That's why apps let you see what is remembered, delete it, turn memory off, or
chat without it, and try not to save sensitive details, such as health, unless
asked.<Cite id="openai-memory" /> Some systems go further and let the model decide what to move in
and out of its prompt as it works, the way a computer moves data between fast memory and
disk.<Cite id="packer2023memgpt" />

</At>
<At levels="deep">

### What notes cost

The notes were 193 tokens from the open request and 221 from the specific one, against 616 for
the whole chat. With the specific notes, the new chat's prompt for Omar's question grew from 25
tokens to 254. Every later chat pays
for its notes, so they have to stay short, and notes saved after every chat pile up into the same
problem as a long chat. The same three answers apply: drop old notes, summarize them, or retrieve
only those that match the question.

</At>

<RecapCard>

- Keyword search finds exact words such as names; search by meaning finds the same idea in other
  words. Many systems run both and merge the results.
- A new chat starts empty. Memory features save notes and put them into later prompts, and a
  wrong note is repeated in every chat after.

<At levels="deep">

- BM25 rewards rare words and repeated use, with diminishing returns; rank fusion adds
  $1/(k + r)$ from each list.

</At>
</RecapCard>

**Next question:** every call re-sends the whole conversation, and the model reads all of it again.
Does it really redo all that work every time?
